---
id: 95b8d9e8-94ae-43b8-818b-99a7814ed42a
title: Inflammatory Bowel Disease
---

# Inflamamatory Bowel Disease

- **Definition** - chronic inflammatory bowel diseases which pursue a protracted relapsing and remitting course over years, with two similar clinical entities:
  - <u>Ulcerative colitis</u> - inflammatory bowel disease resulting in continuous superficial (mucosal and submucosal) lesions extending from the rectum to the ileo-caecal valve
  - <u>Crohn's disease</u> - inflammatory bowel disease resulting in skipped transmural lesions that can involve any part of the GI tract from mouth to anus
- **Epidemiology of IBD:**
  - **Incidence** - increasing incidence of disease in the Western World (after 1950), and subsequently in countries that adopt Westernised lifestyle:
    - <u>Ulcerative colitis</u> (more common) - 10-20/100000/y in the West
    - <u>Crohn's disease</u> - 5-10/100000/y in the West
  - **Demographic** - Peak incidence in 2nd-3rd decade of life, Slight female preponderance in Crohn's disease
- **Comparison between UC and CD:** ![](../img/comparison_between_UC_and_CD_davidson.png) 
![](../img/overview_of_ibd.png)
- **Associated conditions of IBD** - may be unrelated to the bowel disease activity:
  - Autoimmune hepatitis
  - Primary sclerosing cholangitis and cholangiocarcinoma (UC)
  - Seronegative Spondyloarthropanies primarily sacroiliitis and ankylosing spondylitis (CD)
- **Pathophysiology of IBD** - genetic and environmental components:
  - **Genetic factors** - GWAS identify genetic variants (163 susceptibility loci) predisposing to CD have undergone <u>positive natural selection</u> by protection against infectious diseases (e.g. TB):
    - <u>Evidence of genetic predisposition</u>:
      - More common in Ashkenazi Jews
      - +ve FHx - 10% have 1st-degree relative/\>= 1 close relative with IBD
      - High monozygotic concordance rate - 40-50% in CD; 20-25% in UC
    - <u>Implicated genes</u>:
      - **MHC genes** - genetic variants at HLA locus a/w UC and CD
      - **Genes a/w innate immunity and autophagy** (CD) - e.g. NOD2 (a/w ileal and stricturing disease), ATG16L1 and IRGM genes
      - **Defects in barrier function** (UC)
  - **Environmental factors** - environmental triggers in a genetically susceptible individual mounts an abnormal inflammatory result:
    - <u>Smoking</u> - Smoking protective against UC, but risk factor for CD (RR ~ 3)
    - <u>Diet</u> - CD a/w low-residue, high refined-sugar diet
    - <u>Gut microbiome</u> - gut dysbiosis in CD and UC
    - <u>Appendicectomy</u> - protective against UC
  - **Abnormal inflammatory response** - inflammation of intestines w/ release of inflammatory mediators (e.g. TNF, IL-12, IL-23) 
  ![](../img/pathogenesis_of_ibd.png)
  - **Continuous ascending submucosal inflammation in ulcerative colitis:**
    - <u>Anatomical distribution of UC</u> - invariably involves rectum (proctitis), and spreads proximally in continuous manner to involve the colon 
    ![](../img/anatomical_distribution_of_uc.png)
    - <u>Histological distribution of UC</u> - inflammatory process limited to the mucosa:
      - **Cryptitis** - acute and chronic inflammatory infiltrates in the intestinal cryts (crypt abscess is typical)
      - **Loss of goblet cells** - goblet cells lost and glands becomes distorted
      - **Dysplasia** - long-standing inflammation results in cellular atypia and is a risk factor for CRC
    - <u>Bowel shortening and post-inflammatory pseudopolyps</u> - occurs after long-standing pancolitis, where post-inflammatory 'pseudopolyps' are essentially normal mucosa residing within areas of atrophy
  - **Skip lesions and transmural inflammation in Crohn's disease:**
    - <u>Anatomical distribution</u>:
      - Ileal or ileocolonic
      - Smal bowel
      - Colon alone
      - Perianal disease

      
      ![](../img/anatomical_distribution_of_crohns_disease.png)
    - <u>Skip lesions</u> - patchy inflammation disrupted by islands of small bowel
    - <u>Transmural inflammation</u> - resulting in deep ulcers appearing as linear fissures ('cobblestone appearance') with risk of abscess formation and fitsulation
- **Clinical features of IBD:**
  - **Clinical features of ulcerative colitis** - presentation depends on extent and severity of disease, where first attack is typically most severe and takes a <u>relapsing and remitting course</u>:
    - <u>PR bleeding w/ mucus</u> - caused by proctitis, often a/w a sensation of **tenesmus**
    - <u>Altered bowel habits</u> - frequent passage of small volume of loose stool, or pellety stools due to constipation upstream of the inflamed rectum
    - <u>Bloody diarrhoea w/ mucus</u> - due to left-sided colitis or pancolitis a/w **lower abdominal pain**
    - <u>Systemic and toxic Sx</u> - severe attack of IBD is may manifest as fever, tachycardia, peritoneal signs, anorexia, weight loss and malaise
  - **Clinical features of Crohn's disease** - major Sx are 1) abdominal pain, 2) diarrhoea, and 3) weight loss, but dependent on site (Crohn's colitis may mimic UC but rectal sparing and presence of perianal lesions is suggestive of Crohn's):
    - <u>Abdominal pain</u> - periumbilical or lower cramping abdominal pain, depending on extent of involvement during attacks, often a/w diarrhoea (absence may be due to SBO), and <u>provoked by food</u>
    - <u>Diarrhoea</u> - usually **watery w/o of blood or mucus** if isolated ileal or SB disease, but may manifest w/ bloody diarrhoea in Crohn's colitis
    - <u>Weight loss</u> - attributable to anorexia because abdominal pain is provoked by food, but can be related to **malabsorption** (presents w/ features suggestive of fat, protein, or vitamin deficiencies)
    - <u>Severe vomiting</u> - if manifests as IO or resulting from jejunal strictures
  - **Extraintestinal features** - see complications
- **Signs of IBD:**
  - <u>Geneeral examination</u> - weight loss, pallor w/ glossitis and angular stomatitis (B12 deficiency)
  - <u>Abdominal examination</u>:
    - Tenderness (most marked over inflammed areas)
    - +/- peritoneal signs in severe colitis
    - Abdominal mass - matted loops of thickened bowel
  - <u>PR examination</u> - perianal skin tags, fissures, or fistulaes (50%)
- **Complications of IBD:**
  - **Life-threatening colonic inflammation** - typically in the first attack of ulcerative or Crohn's colitis:
    - <u>Pathophysiology</u> - colon dilates (**toxic megacolon**) with bacterial toxins passing freely into circulation and resulting in sepsis
    - <u>Clinical features</u> - systemic and toxic features dominate: 
    ![](../img/severity_of_colitis.png)
    - <u>Mx</u> - medical Tx + daily AXR to r/o toxic megacolon (transverse \> 6cm) due to risk of colonic perforation
  - **Haemorrhage** - eroision into major artery is rare but can occur in both conditions, resulting in massive LGIB
  - **Fistulae** - specific to Crohn's disease due to <u>transmural inflammation</u>:
    - <u>Enteroenteric fistulation</u> - results in **blind loop syndrome**, manifesting as malabsorption, and diarrhoea
    - <u>Enterovesical fistulation</u> - results in recurrent cystitis and UTI, and **pneumaturia**
    - <u>Enterovaginal fistula</u> - caused by faeculent vaginal discharge
  - **Cancer** - risk increases w/ <u>duration and extent</u> of <u>uncontrolled colonic inflammation</u>:
    - <u>Pathophysiology</u> - long-standing inflammation results in dysplasia and constitutes risk of CRC
    - <u>Cumulative risk for dysplasia</u>:
      - **Ulcerative colitis** - up to 20% after 30y
      - **Crohn's colitis** - lower than in ulcerative colitis
    - <u>Association w/ primary sclerosing cholangitis</u> (PSC):
      - Increased risk of CRC and subsequently CC for unknown regions
    - <u>Mx</u>:
      - **Medical Tx** (e.g. mesalazine, azathioprine) - a/w reduced risk of dysplasia in both conditions likely due to sustained healing of colonic mucosa
      - **Surveillance programmes** - entered after long-standing colitis (10y):
        - <u>Technique</u> - pancolonic chromo-endoscopy through staining w/ indigo carmine or methylene blue to detect areas for Bx (superceded standard colonoscopy w/ random Bx)
        - <u>Risk stratification by histology</u> - into high, medium, and low risk groups to determine surveillance interval or opt for panproctocolectomy
      - **Panproctocolectomy** - recommended for high-risk groups
  - **Extraintestinal manifestations** - some occur only during active phase of disease, while some are unrelated to disease activity: 
  ![](../img/extraintestinal_manifestation_of_ibd.png)
- **Extraintestinal manifestations** (EIMs):
  - **Overview:**
    - Up to 33% of IBD patients have at least one extra-intestinal disease manifestations, some <u>parallels with</u>, others <u>unrelated to</u> bowel disease activity
    - Explanations for EIMs include 1) disease pathophysiology, 2) Tx-related complications, or 3) simply by association
  - **Classification of EIMs in IBDs** - by 1) organ system involvement, or 2) by clinical course:
    - <u>Rheumatoligical disorders</u> - peripheral arthritis, sacroiliitis, ankylosing spondylitis
    - <u>Metabolic disorders</u> (related to GC exposure) - osteoproosis, avascular necrosis
    - <u>Dermatological disorders</u> - erythema nodosum, pyodermal gangrenosum, psoriaisis, sweet syndrome, aphthous stomatitis
    - <u>Occular disorders</u> - scleritis and episcleritis, uveiitis
    - <u>Hepatobiliary disorders</u> - fatty liver, choledocholithiasis, primary sclerosing cholangitis, cholangiocarcinoma
    - <u>Urological disorders</u> - nephrolithiasis

    
    ![](../img/eim_in_ibd_harrisons.png)
  - **Dermatological disorders:**
    - <u>Erythema nodosum</u> (EN) - red, tender, 1-5 cm lesions, symmetrical nodules, commonly found on the shin, ankles, calves, thighs, and arms:
      - Occurs in 15% of CD patients and 10% of UC patients
      - Attacks usually correlate w/ bowel activity, w/ skin lesions developing after the onset of bowel Sx
      - Patients often have concominent migratory arthritis
      - Therapy is directed towards underlying bowel disease
    - <u>Pyoderma gangrenosum</u> (PG) - pustular lesions (up to 30cm) that ulcerate w/ violaceous edges surrounded by a margin of erythema, spreading concentrically; commonly affecting the dorsum of the foot, but also the legs, chest, stoma, or even the face:
      - Occurs in 1-12% of UC patients and much less common in Crohn's colitis
      - Attacks are unrelated to bowel activity, and may occur independently before or after onset to disease, and responds poorly w/ colectomy
      - Therapy requires IV Abx to prevent secondary infections, as well as heavy immunosuppressants and biologics
    - <u>Other dermatological manifestations</u>:
      - Pyoderma vegetans (intertriginous areas)
      - Pyostomatitis vegetans (mucous memranes)
      - Sweet syndrome (neutrophilic dermatosis)
      - Metastatic CD (skin granuloma formations)
      - Oromucosal manifestations (apthous stomatitis, cobblestone lesions of buccal mucosa)
      - Psoriasis
  - **Rheumatological disorders:**
    - <u>Peripheral arthritis</u> (10-20%; more common in CD) - asymetrical, polyarticular, and migratory often affecting the large joints, often correlating w/ IBD disease activity
    - <u>Seronegative Spondyloarthropathies</u> (10% develop AS) - a/w HLA-B27 positivity; sacroillitis often asymptomatic and does not corerlate w/ disease activity, where some develop to AS
    - <u>Other rheumatological manifestations</u>:
      - Hypertrophic osteoarthropathy
      - Pelvic/ femoral osteomyelitis
      - Relapsing polychondritis
  - **Occular disorders:**
    - <u>Conjunctivitis</u> - non-specific manifestation unrelated to disease activity
    - <u>Anterior uveitis</u> - a/w both IBDs and unrelated to disease activity, presenting w/ acute unilateral BOV, occular pain, photophobia, and headaches, and can be sight threatening
    - <u>Episcleritis</u> - benign condition w/ mildly painful red eye a/w autoimmune disorders
  - **Hepatobiliary disorders:**
    - <u>Hepatic steatosis</u> (\> 50%) - asymptomataic, usually w/ hepatomegaly, due to chronic debilitating illness, malnutrition, and glucocorticoid therapy
    - <u>Choledocholithiasis</u> (10-35%) - due to impaired bile salt reasborption w/ disease involvement of the ileum (or w/ ileal resection)
    - <u>Primary sclerosing cholangitis</u> - 5% of IBD patients have PSC; 75% of PSC patients have IBD:
      - Both IBD and PSC may have similar immunogenetic basis as are commonly pANCA +ve
      - Generally identified after IBD diagnosis possibly years after colectomy
      - A/w multifocal strictures and dilatation of the entire biliary tree (small duct primary sclerosing cholangitis primarily affects small ducts and may be a/w better prognosis than classical PSC)
      - Most commonly asymptomatic, but may present w/ non-specific constitutional Sx, jaundice, and abdominal pain
      - Complications include biliary cirrhosis, and cholangiocarcinoma (dominant stricture)
    - <u>Gallbladder polyposis and gallbladder malignancy</u> - high incidence of malignancy w/ PSC
  - **Urologic disorders** - nephrolithiasis w/ <u>calcium oxalate stones</u>:
    - Normally oxalate in diet combines w/ dietary calcium to form insoluble calcium oxalate, which is eliminated in stool
    - In patients w/ ileal dysfunction, non-absorbed fatty acid combines w/ dietary calcium, leading to luminal oxalate unbound, which is absorbed in colon (esp. during inflammation)
    - This results in hyperoxaluria and increased risk of calcium oxalate stones
- **Ix** - confirm Dx, define disease distribution and severity, and detect complications:
  - **Routine bloods** - CBC, LRFT, ESR/CRP, +/- folate, B12:
    - <u>CBC</u> - anaemia due to 1) bleeding, 2) malabsorption of iron, folate or B12
    - <u>LFT</u> - hypoalbuminaemia due to 1) -ve actute phase response, 2) poor nutrition, 3) exudative protein loss, 4) malnutrition
    - <u>RFT</u> - baseline for CT, and administration of DMARDs
    - <u>ESR/CRP</u> - non-specifically elevated in exacerbations (ESR typically not raised in UC; raised might suggest an infection)
    - <u>B12/ folate</u> - deficiency due to malabsorption
  - **Stool** - faecal calprotectin, stool for microbiology x3:
    - <u>Faecal calprotectin</u> - elevated reflecting gastrointestinal inflammation even if CRP normal (for distinguishing IBS and IBD)
    - <u>Microbiology</u> (send stool for microscopy, culture, examination for C diff toxins, ova and cysts) - exclude superimposed GI infections
  - **Endoscopy:**
    - **Ileocolonoscomy or sigmoidoscopy** - ileocolonoscomy preferred; sigmoidoscopy sufficient for Dx in acute severe episodes:
      - <u>Indications</u> - chronic diarrhoea w/ raised inflammatory markers or alarm features such as weight loss, rectal bleeding, anaemia
      - <u>Diagnostic findings</u> - Bx from each anatomical segment to define disease extent and detect dyspasia:
        - Ulcerative colitis - loss of vascular pattern, granularity, friability and contact bleeding +/- ulcerations (or complicating carcinoma)
        - Crohn's disease - rectal sparing, with patchy inflammation with discrete, deep ulcers, pseudopolyp formation and strictures (+/- complicating carcinoma)
  - **OGD** - for all children and most adults w/ Crohn's to complete staging
  - **SB workup** - for suspected Crohn's disease:
    - <u>Video capsule enteroscopy</u> - indicated if absence of strictures
    - <u>Enteroscopy</u> - required for histological diagnosis of small bowel involvement
  - **Radiology:**
    - **AXR** - indicate for acute severe disease:
      - <u>Dilatation</u> - detection of toxic megalcolon (transverse \> 6cm) impending perforation
      - <u>Mucosal oedema</u> - thumbprinting
      - <u>Pneumoperitoneum</u> - evidence of perforation
      - <u>IO</u> - detection of IO in those presenting w/ suspected IO due to Crohn's strictures
    - **Barium studies** - follow-through or enema studies are now not preferred: 
    ![](../img/barrium_swallow_for_ibd.png)
    - **CT colonogram** - if colonoscopy incomplete
    - **MRI enterography** - high Sn for extraintestinal, pelvic and perineal involvement
- **Dx** - requires endoscopic and histological Dx

## Differential Diagnoses of IBD

- **DDx for small bowel Crohn's disease:**
  - <u>Small bowel infection</u> - tuberculosis, Yersinia, Actinomycosis
  - <u>Other causes of right liac fossa mass</u> - caecal carcinoma, appendiceal absess, mesenteric adenitis
  - <u>Others</u> - pelvic inflammatory disease, SB lymphoma

  
  ![](../img/conditions_mimicking_small_bowel_crohns.png)

<!-- -->

- **DDx of UC and CD:**
  - <u>Infectious diseases</u> - bacterial, fungal, viral, or protozoal
  - <u>Non-infectious diseases</u> - other inflammatory conditions such as diverticulitis, ischeamic colitis, radiation colitis, drug-induced colitis, neoplastic conditions
  - <u>Atypical colitides</u> - normal endoscopic apperance w/ abnormal histology, namely collagenous colitis, and lymphocytic colitis ![](../img/conditions_mimicking_crohns_or_ulcerative_colitis.png) 
  ![](../img/diseases_that_mimic_ibd.png)
- **Infectious diseases mimicking IBD:**
  - **Bacterial acute gastroenteritis:**
    - <u>Campylobacter colitis</u> - manifestations of acute dysentery w/ systemic features, and can **mimic the endoscopic appearance of UC**, ocassionally responsible for suspected relapse of UC
    - <u>Salmonella</u> - watery or bloody diarrhoea w/ N/V, usually self-limiting but 1% become chronic cariers
    - <u>Shigellosis</u> - manifestation w/ watery diarrhoea, abdominal pain, and fever, followed by rectal tenesmus and passage of blood and mucus per rectum
    - <u>Yersinia enterolitica</u> - predominant manifestation in the **terminal ileum** and can cause mucosal ulceration and thickening of ileal wall
    - <u>Clostridium difficile</u> - a/w ABx use, presenting w/ watery diarrhoea, tenesmus, N/V
    - <u>E. coli</u> - all three categories, i.e. enterohaemorrhagic, enteroinvasive, and enteroadherent forms can manifest as as bloody diarrhoea, and abdominal tenderness
    - <u>Sexually transmitted disease</u> - **acute proctitis** due to 1) gonorrhoea, 2) Chlamydia trachomatis, and 3) Syphilis
  - **Mycobacterial ileitis and colitis** (MTB, MAC complexes) - primarily occurs in the immunocompromised patients, but ocassionally in the an immunocompetent host:
    - <u>Site of infections</u> - typically distal ileum and caecum involvement
    - <u>Clinical manifestations</u>:
      - Altered bowel habits (may be unnoticeable)
      - Small bowel obstruction
      - Tender abdominal mass
    - <u>Dx</u> - colonosocopy w/ Bx and w/ AFB smear and C/ST
  - **Viral colitis** - common viral GE rarely get mistakened for IBD but are limited to small bowel involvement, while viral colitis by herpesviridae may mimick UC or Crohn's colitis:
    - **CMV colitis:**
      - <u>Demographic</u> - in immunocomprimised individuals but CMV proctitis can occur in immunocompetent adults
      - <u>Site of involvement</u> - GI involvement:
        - Most commonly affecting the colon, rectum, and esophagus
        - Small bowel involvement may occur
      - <u>Clinical manifestations</u> - sub-acute to chronic presentation w/ GI and constitutional Sx:
        - Abdominal pain
        - Bloody diarrhoea
        - Fever
        - Weight loss
      - <u>Dx</u> - demonstration of **inclusion bodies** (intranuclear inclusions) in mucosa cells on Bx
      - <u>Mx</u> - IV gancyclovir or PO valgancyclovir
    - **HSV colitis:**
      - <u>Demographic</u>:
        - Oropharyngeal and anorectal involvement can occur in immunocompetent individuals who have risk factors for acquiring the infection
        - Colonic involvement only occurs in immunocomprimised individuals
      - <u>GI involvement</u>:
        - Oropharynx
        - Colon
        - Anorectal region
        - Perianal region
      - <u>Clinical manifestations</u> - usually diarrhoea is rare, while most present w/ anorectal manifestations:
        - Anorectal pain and tenesmus
        - Constipation
        - Inguinal adenopathy
        - Difficulty w/ urinary voiding (as a/w pain)
        - Sacral parasthesia
    - **HIV infections** - infection of SB can result in diarrhoea, N/V, and anorexia w/ SIBO and fat malabsorption
  - **Parasitic infections:**
    - <u>Isopora belli</u> - acute, self-limiting GE in immunocompetite hosts, while causing chronic watery diarrhoea in immunocomprimised hosts
    - <u>Entamoeba histolytica and related species</u> - manifestation as abdominal pain, tenesmus w/ frequent loose stools containing blood and mucus, macroscopically resulting in **focal punctate ulcer w/ normal intervening mucosa** mimicking Crohn's colitis
    - <u>Other parasitic infections</u> - hookworms, whipworms, strongyloides stercoralis, disseminated histoplasmosis
- **Non-infectious enteritis and colitis:**
  - **Diverticulitis** - easily confused w/ Crohn's disease clinically and radiographically
  - **Ischaemic colitis** - acute mesenteric ischaemia may mimick disease flares, while chronic mesenteric ischaemia mimicks indolent phases of the disease course:
    - **Acute mesenteric ischaemia** - acute ischaemia usually in the watershed area of the large bowel:
      - <u>Risk factors</u> - considered in elderly w/ vasculopathic risk factors (severe CAD, PAD), hypercoagulable states (e.g. malignancy), atrial fibrillation, or after repair of abdominal aortic aneurysm
      - <u>Clinical manifestations</u> - a/w acute ischaemia of the water-shed area:
        - LLQ abdominal pain
        - PR bleeding (usually bright red blood per rectum)
        - Tenesmus and urgency to defacate
      - <u>Endoscopic features of acute mesenteric ischaemia</u> - sharp transition between rectum and the ischaemic water-shed zone:
        - Relatively normal mucosa of the rectum
        - Area of inflammation at the descending colon and the splenic flexures
        - However, inflammation tends to resolve quickly
    - **Chronic mesenteric ischaemia:**
  - **Radiation enteritis, colitis or proctitis** - biphasic effects w/ acute mucosal inflammation and chronic mucosal involement:
    - **Clinical manifestations:**
      - <u>Effects of acute irradiation</u> - typically occurs within 1-2 weeks after irradiation:
        - Acute radiation proctitis - diarrhoea w/ blood and mucous, tenesmus, mimicking distal UC
        - Acute radiation enteritis - watery diarrhoea
      - <u>Affects of chronic irradiation</u> - after years complicated w/ malabsorption, strictures, and fistulations as in CD
    - **Endoscopic findings** - mucosal granularity, friability, numerous telangiectasia and ocassionally ulcerations
  - **Drug-induced enteritis and colitis:**
    - <u>NSAID-associated intestinal toxicity</u> - various manifestations from mucosal irritation, ulcerations, strictures and fistulisations. mimicking usually CD, but is also **known to cause reactivation of IBD**
    - <u>ICI-related colitis</u> - common IRAE (21-44% patients) usually presents w/ self-limited diarrhoea but can be associated w/ frank colitis
- **Atypical colitides** - colitides w/ abnormal histopathology explaining Sx despite normal endoscopic appearance:
  - <u>Collagenous colitis</u> - typically presents w/ watery diarrhoea in F in 6-7th decade of life w/ known risk factors:
    - Risk factors include 1) smoking, 2) Hx of NSAID, PPI, or beta-blocker use use, and 3) Hx of autoimmune disease
    - Demographic shows female preponderance (F:M 9:1) and onset in 6-7th decade of life
    - Defined by two main histological components: 1) increased subepithelial collagen deposition, 2) increased intraepithelial lymphocytes
  - <u>Lymphocytic colitis</u> - typically presents w/ watery diarrhoea:
    - Risk factors similar to collagenous colitis, but additionally a/w use of steraline and not beta-blockers
    - Demographic shows no sex preponderance (M=F) and onset in 6-7th decade of life
    - Absence of subepithelial collagen deposition

## Management of IBD

- **Principles of Mx** - requires establishing a multidisciplinary team-based approach to tackle disease and related complications:
  - <u>Medical Tx</u> - treatment of acute attacks (induce remission) and prevent relapse (maintain remission), preventing bowel damage, dysplasia and carcinoma
  - <u>Surgical Tx</u> - for those w/ complications
- **Drugs used for medical Tx of IBD:** 
![](../img/drugs_used_in_tx_of_ibd.png)
- **Medical Tx of ulcerative colitis:**
  - <u>Acute proctitis</u> - treated w/:
    - 1g mesalazine suppository (1st line)
    - Oral 5-aminosalicylate (5-ASA) - add on therapy if non-ersponsive to suppository
    - Systemic corticosteroids or immunosuppressants - considered if resistant
  - <u>Mild-to-moderate active left-sided or extensive ulcerative colitis</u> - typically require admission for Dx but subsequent attacks can be treated in outpatient setting:
    - Top and tail approach - oral and topical 5-ASA preparation (topical preperation is withdrawn at 1 mo with oral preperation continued as maintenance therapy)
    - Oral prednisolone (40 mg/d tapered by 5mg/w for 8w) - considered if no response to top and tail approach
    - Add on thiopurine - at first sign of corticosteroid resistance or those requiring high doses of steroids to maintain control
  - <u>Severe ulcerative colitis</u> - admission for patients failing oral therapy and those presenting severe UC by Truelove-Witts criteria:
    - **Monitoring:**
      - <u>Clinical monitoring</u> - for presence of abdominal pain, temperature, pulse rate, stool blood and frequency
      - <u>Laboratory</u> - CBC, LRFT, inflammatory markers
      - <u>Radiological</u> - daily AXR for colonic dilatation
    - **General measures:**
      - Correct fluids and electroytes
      - Enteral nutritional support for malnourished patients
    - **IV corticosteroids** - 60mg methylprednisolone or 400mg/d hydrocortisone and assess clinical response in first 3d
    - **Medical rescue therapy** - for those unresponsive to steroids to avoid urgent colectomy:
      - Infliximab (5mg/kg)
      - Cyclosporin (2mg/kg)

    
    ![](../img/medical_tx_of_fulminant_uc.png)
  - <u>Maintenance therapy</u> - lifelong maintenance for those w/ left-sided or extensive disease:
    - Once daily oral 5-ASA (1st line agent)
    - Sulfasalazine as alternative
- **Medical Tx of Crohn's disease:**
  - <u>Induction of remission</u>:
    - **Corticosteroid therapy** - with vitamin D and Ca supplements to negate inhibitory effect on intestinal Ca absorption:
      - <u>Budesonide</u> (9mg od for 6w and taperring over 2w - indicated for ileal disease due to 90% first-pass metabolism and thus less systemic S/E
      - <u>Prednisolone</u> (40mg od for 8w taperring by 5mg/w) - if not responsive to budesonide
    - **Elemental or polymeric diet** - as bridge to definitive Tx prior to urgent staging Ix at first presentation
    - **Induction therapy w/ anti-TNF agent and thiopurine** (infliximab, adalimumab, azathioprine) - for severe colonic disease requiring IV corticosteroids:
      - Evidence showed increased efficacy) for inducing and maintaing remission than anti-TNF monotherapy and thiopurine therapy
  - <u>Maintenance therapy</u>:
    - Thiopurines - azathioprine, mercaptopurine
    - Anti-TNF agent - infliximab, adalimumab
    - Methotrexate
- **Surgical Tx for ulcerative colitis** - Tx of curative intent by removing all parts of the bowel that can potentially be affected by UC:
  - <u>Procedures</u>:
    - Panproctocolectomy with ileostomy
    - Proctocolectomy with ileal-anal pouch anastamosis
  - <u>Indications for surgery</u> - generally if affecting QOL, failure of medical therapy, or complicated w/ colon cancer: 
  ![](../img/indications_for_proctocolectomy_in_uc.png)
- **Surgical Tx of Crohn's disease** - Tx is non-curative, and <u>disease recurrence is the rule</u>:
  - <u>Approach</u> - conservative approach to minimise loss of viable intestine and to avoid creation of short gut syndrome:
    - Segmental resection
    - Stricturoplasty (incisision of strictures in longitudinal axis)
    - Total colectomy and end-iliostomy (ileal-anal pouch avoided as recurrence is common within the pouch resulting in abscess formation, fistulation, and subsequent pouch failure)
  - <u>Advice to avoid post-operative recurrence</u> - most often require revisional surgery at some point in time:
    - Smoking cessation is the only factor that has consistently shown to reduce recurrence
    - Aggressive medical therapy w/ intense monitoring
    - Repeat endoscopy and Bx of anastamotic sites (endoscopic recurrence subsequently require thiopurines)
- **5-ASA:**
  - **Oral 5-ASA formulations:**
  - **MOA** - not fully known:
    - Mediate activity through peroxisome proliferator-activator receptor gamma (PPAR-gamma)
    - Decreased nuclear localisation of NF-kappaB (master gene of inflammation)
  - **Formulations and dosing** - most mild-to-moderate UC typically respond to 5-ASA doses equivalent to 2 g/d of mesalamine, w/ dose response continuing up to 4.8 g/d: 
  ![](../img/oral_formulations_of_5_asa.png)
  - **Pharmacokinetics of different 5-ASA preparations** - different properties of preparations results in delievery of mesalamine to different parts of the GI tract:
  - **S/E** - annual assessment of RFT and urinalysis required:
    - <u>Non-specific S/E</u> - headaches, hair loss
    - <u>GI disturbances</u> - nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain
    - <u>Paradoxical worsening of colitis</u> - thought to be mediated by an idiosyncratic hypersensitivity reaction
    - <u>Renal impairments</u> - worsening CrCl w/ haematuria
  - **Topical or rectal 5-ASA suppositories** - as monotherapy for mild rectal UC or as combination therapy:
  - <u>Topical Rowasa enemas</u> - topical enema composed w/ mesalamine
  - <u>Canasa</u> - suppositories composed of mesalamine effective as monotherapy for proctitis
- **Biologics and small moecules used in Mx of IBD:** 
![](../img/biologics_and_small_molecules_used_in_tx_of_ibd.png)
  - **Anti-TNF therapies:**
    - **Selections:**
      - <u>Infliximab</u> (prototype) - chimeric IgG Ab against TNF-alpha, approved for moederate-to-severe **CD** and **UC**, and fistulizing CD
      - <u>Adalimumab</u> - recombinat human monoclonal IgG1 Ab containing only human peptide sequences injecteed subcutaneously, approved for moderate-to-severe CD and UC (better for patients naive to anti-TNF than previously Tx w/ inflixamib)
      - <u>Certolizumab pegol</u> - pegylated form of anti-TNF FAb portion of the Ab, only approved for **moderate-severe CD**
      - <u>Golimumab</u> - fully human IgG Ab against TNF-alpha, only approved for the Tx of **moderate-to-severe CD**
    - **MOA** - TNF-alpha blockade
    - **S/E:**
      - <u>Infections or reactivation of latent infections</u> - reactivation of latent TB (screened via IGRA, NOT CXR), opportunistic fungal infections (including disseminated histoplasmosis and coccidiomycosis)
      - <u>Hepatotoxicity</u> - rarely due to reactivation of HBV, autoimmune effects, and cholestasis
      - <u>Lymphoma</u> - slightly increased risk of NHL and hepatosplenic T-cell lymphoma (HSTCL) reported through case reports, unsure whether related to thiopurine usage or anti-TNF alpha (a Danish cohort study showed no increased risk after adjustment for co-treatments)
      - <u>Development of Ab against anti-TNF alpha</u> - a/w **infusion reactions**, and **decreased response to Tx**:
        - Hence current practices is for on-demand or episodic infusions rather than periodic infusions to lower risk of developing antibodies
        - Immunomodulators (e.g. AZA, MP, MTX) added to anti-TNF help prevent antibody formation
        - Commercial assays and measurements of anti-infliximab or ADA Ab, trough levels of infliximab to guide whether intra- or inter-class switch is necessary
      - <u>Infusion reactions</u> - a/w serum sickness may be managed w/ glucocorticoids and anti-histamines
  - **Anti-integrins:**
    - **Selection:**
      - <u>Natalizumab</u> - recombinant human IgG4 against alpha-4 integrin now withdrawn due to risk of PML
      - <u>Vedolizumab</u> - monoclonal Ab against alpha-4-beta-7 integrin that conveys gut-selective immunosuppression, used for first-line therapy or after failure of anti-TNF in **moderate-to-severe UC**
    - **MOA of vedolizumab** - gut-selective immunosuppression:
      - <u>Inhibition of GI inflammation</u> - through inhibition of adhesion of discrete gut homing subset of T lymphocyte to MAdCAM-1, which is specific for lymphocyte trafficking in the gut mucosa
      - <u>No effect of system lymphocyte trafficking</u> - limited effects on vascular adhesion moelcule 1 (VAM1), hence lymphocyte traficking in systemic tissues (esp. CNS and hence lower risk of PML)
    - **S/E** - benefits being reduced theoretical risk of systemic opportunistic infections and malignancies:
      - Nasopharyngitis
      - Headaches
      - Arthralgia
      - Nausea
  - **Biologics influencing interleukin signalling** (IL-12 and IL-13):
    - **Selection and MOA** - IL12 and IL23 share common p40 subunits, w/ p19 and p35 respectively:
      - <u>Uztekinumab</u> - p40 inhibitor (IgG Ab), resulting in inhibition of both IL-12 and IL-23 signalling pathways, indicated for **moderate-to-severe UC** (attractive if concomitent psoriatic arthritis)
      - <u>Risakizumab</u> - p19 inhibitor (MAb), resulting in inhibition of IL-23 signalling
  - **Small molecules for IBD** - reduced immunogenicity and advantage of being able to diffuse through cell membranes into intracellular space and ihibit cytokine signalling pathway:
  - **JAK inhibitors** - usually as second-line agents for moderate-to-severe UC and CD:
    - **Selection and MOA:**
      - <u>Tofacitinib</u> - competition w/ ATP preventing **phosphorylation and activation of JAK**, resulting in **pan-JAK inhibitions** (although more potent for JAK 1 and JAK3, raises concerns for safety)
      - <u>Upadacitinib</u> - selective, reversibel inhibitor of JAK1 (potentially better safety profile)
    - **S/E:**
      - Increased risk of cardiovascular events
      - Increased risk of malignancy
      - Increased risk of viral, bacterial, and fungal invasions
      - Dyslipidaemia
      - Hepatotoxicity
      - Marrow suppression
